  "dom_id": "step-q211465",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939872/assets/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of \n$y=-f(x-1)-2?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the right, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $2$ units down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **minimum** of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-1)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+1,3)=(3,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1\\cdot 3)=(3,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-3-2)=(3,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ is $(3,-5).$",
